% M2-L10. THE MULTI-LEVEL SHAPE: backjump_unsat with a third spectator, so a single
% refutation skips THREE sibling branches in a row rather than two.
%
% Same core contradiction as backjump_unsat.fzn -- x <> y and x = y over 0..3, invisible
% to bounds reasoning, so the refutation has to branch down x's ladder and close both
% children at every step. p, q and r are unconstrained and share x's and y's smallest
% domain width (0..1), so first-fail (ties broken by declaration order, docs/SPEC.md 3.4)
% branches on them FIRST, one level each, ahead of x and y.
%
% Every conflict's decision closure names x's level alone (none of p, q or r's), so the
% frames owning all THREE of their levels find a nogood already false without their own
% decision and skip their sibling -- one backjump event per frame, so a SINGLE refutation
% crosses three levels rather than backjump_unsat's two. Measured 2026-09-18:
%
%     learning off   63 nodes, 31 decisions, 0 skipped
%     learning on     7 nodes,  4 decisions, 3 skipped
%
% Learned clauses are over x's and y's order literals, a threshold strictly inside their
% ladder, so this is the NON-convertible path (D-0044's fork ii): Learned.to_linear_row
% returns None on every one of them, same as backjump_unsat.
%
% Widths are 0..1 (p, q, r) and 0..3 (x, y): D-0028, the order encoding is
% width-proportional.
var 0..1: p :: output_var;
var 0..1: q :: output_var;
var 0..1: r :: output_var;
var 0..3: x :: output_var;
var 0..3: y :: output_var;
constraint int_ne(x, y);
constraint int_eq(x, y);
solve satisfy;
